Most characters in `s` may not appear in `t`. This version first keeps only the positions that can help. The sliding window then runs over that smaller list, while the saved positions still let us return the correct part of the original string.

This does not improve the worst case, because every character may be relevant. It can be handy when `s` is large and `t` contains only a few different characters.

```python
class Solution:
    def minWindow(self, s: str, t: str) -> str:
        if not s or not t or len(s) < len(t):
            return ""

        needed = {}
        for char in t:
            needed[char] = needed.get(char, 0) + 1

        positions = []
        characters = []
        for index, char in enumerate(s):
            if char in needed:
                positions.append(index)
                characters.append(char)

        if len(characters) < len(t):
            return ""

        remaining = needed.copy()
        missing = len(t)
        left = 0
        best_start = 0
        best_length = float("inf")

        for right, char in enumerate(characters):
            if remaining[char] > 0:
                missing -= 1
            remaining[char] -= 1

            while missing == 0:
                start_position = positions[left]
                end_position = positions[right]
                length = end_position - start_position + 1

                if length < best_length:
                    best_start = start_position
                    best_length = length

                left_char = characters[left]
                remaining[left_char] += 1
                if remaining[left_char] > 0:
                    missing += 1
                left += 1

        if best_length == float("inf"):
            return ""
        return s[best_start:best_start + best_length]
```

For `s = "a"` and `t = "aa"`, the filtered list has only one character, so the method returns `""`. That duplicate requirement is why checking only distinct characters would be incorrect.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`. Space complexity: `O(n + k)` because the useful positions and character list are stored.

Here the map keeps the target counts and is adjusted as the window changes. `matched` counts individual target characters, so repeated letters are not missed. The window is valid only when `matched` reaches `t.size()`.

The answer stores just the start index and length. That avoids creating many temporary substrings while scanning.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {
        if (s.empty() || t.empty() || s.size() < t.size()) {
            return "";
        }

        unordered_map<char, int> remaining;
        for (char c : t) {
            remaining[c]++;
        }

        int left = 0;
        int matched = 0;
        int bestStart = 0;
        int bestLength = INT_MAX;

        for (int right = 0; right < static_cast<int>(s.size()); right++) {
            char current = s[right];

            if (remaining[current] > 0) {
                matched++;
            }
            remaining[current]--;

            while (matched == static_cast<int>(t.size())) {
                int length = right - left + 1;
                if (length < bestLength) {
                    bestLength = length;
                    bestStart = left;
                }

                char removed = s[left];
                remaining[removed]++;
                if (remaining[removed] > 0) {
                    matched--;
                }
                left++;
            }
        }

        if (bestLength == INT_MAX) {
            return "";
        }
        return s.substr(bestStart, bestLength);
    }
};
```

An empty target returns an empty string, and a target longer than `s` cannot fit. The input string is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` on average with the hash map. Space complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of stored characters.

This is another simple baseline. It starts each window at every index, keeps a count of the target characters still missing, and stops extending that start as soon as a valid window appears. Since the first valid end is the shortest window for that fixed start, there is no need to inspect longer windows from the same position.

A target such as `"AA"` works normally because `missing` begins at two and only decreases when both copies are found.

```java
class Solution {
    public String minWindow(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() == 0 || t.length() == 0 || s.length() < t.length()) {
            return "";
        }

        int answerStart = 0;
        int answerLength = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int left = 0; left < s.length(); left++) {
            int[] count = new int[128];
            for (int i = 0; i < t.length(); i++) {
                count[t.charAt(i)]++;
            }

            int missing = t.length();

            for (int right = left; right < s.length(); right++) {
                char current = s.charAt(right);

                if (count[current] > 0) {
                    missing--;
                }
                count[current]--;

                if (missing == 0) {
                    int length = right - left + 1;
                    if (length < answerLength) {
                        answerStart = left;
                        answerLength = length;
                    }
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return answerLength == Integer.MAX_VALUE
                ? ""
                : s.substring(answerStart, answerStart + answerLength);
    }
}
```

For `s = "a"` and `t = "b"`, no window becomes valid, so the initial answer marker is still untouched and the method returns an empty string.

Time complexity: `O(n * (n + m))`, because the target counts are rebuilt for every starting position. Space complexity: `O(1)` with the fixed character array.

The window grows from the right until no target characters are missing. Then the left side moves forward, removing extra characters and keeping the window as short as possible.

The dictionary stores remaining counts. A negative value means the window has extra copies, so those characters can be removed safely. For example, with `t = "AABC"`, the window must keep two `A` characters, not just one.

