For `s = "ADOBECODEBANC"` and `t = "ABC"`, the first valid window is `"ADOBEC"`. Shrinking and continuing later produces `"BANC"`, which is shorter.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`. Each pointer only moves forward. Space complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of characters stored in the dictionary.

A direct version can be useful for checking the idea first. For every possible starting position, extend the window to the right and reduce the remaining character counts. As soon as the window contains all of `t`, save it and move to the next start.

This is slower than the usual two-pointer version, but it is easy to follow. Duplicate characters in `t` are handled because the count starts with the full target frequency.

```java
class Solution {
    public String minWindow(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() == 0 || t.length() == 0 || s.length() < t.length()) {
            return "";
        }

        int bestStart = -1;
        int bestLength = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int start = 0; start < s.length(); start++) {
            int[] remaining = new int[128];
            for (int i = 0; i < t.length(); i++) {
                remaining[t.charAt(i)]++;
            }

            int missing = t.length();

            for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
                char current = s.charAt(end);

                if (remaining[current] > 0) {
                    missing--;
                }
                remaining[current]--;

                if (missing == 0) {
                    int length = end - start + 1;
                    if (length < bestLength) {
                        bestLength = length;
                        bestStart = start;
                    }
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return bestStart == -1 ? "" : s.substring(bestStart, bestStart + bestLength);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * (n + m))`, where `n` is the length of `s` and `m` is the length of `t`. Space complexity: `O(1)` for the fixed-size character array.

